00:17:31.000 --> 00:18:24.000
So one of the classic studies finds that if you're a white college freshman and you're randomly assigned to a black roommate rather than a white roommate, your implicit racial biases will go down over the course of your first semester in college. So that shows at least while you're having some of these sustained everyday contact experiences, your implicit racial biases are changing in response. But oftentimes when we do find evidence like this, it really is day-in-day-out experience, which a lot of us, for a lot of us, is either difficult to do, motivationally or very difficult to do because we live in a racially segregated society. And so it's not easy to get contact, especially the type of high-quality contact with people of different backgrounds that we need. Where do our implicit biases come from?
因此，一项经典研究发现，如果你是一个白人大学新生，并且被随机分配到一个黑人室友而不是白人室友，你在大学第一学期期间的种族内隐偏见就会下降。这表明，至少当你有一些持续的日常接触经历时，你的种族内隐偏见正在发生改变以作出回应。但通常当我们发现这样的证据时，它确实是日复一日的经历，而这对于我们许多人来说，要么由于动机原因很难做到，要么因为我们生活在一个按种族隔离的社会中而非常难以做到。因此，获得接触并不容易，尤其是我们需要的那种与不同背景的人的高质量接触。我们的内隐偏见从何而来？

22
00:18:24.000 --> 00:19:25.000
What we know is they have two common sources. One is they are in large part reflections of our knowledge and internalization of societal hierarchies at large, of who has more power, who has more status, who is more positively regarded within a society. And so this is part of why they may often diverge from our explicit biases, right? When we are seeing all of these stereotypes reinforcing certain racial hierarchies, such that, you know, white folks are the ones that are powerful and high status and admired and black folks are dangerous and criminal and violent, there's an imprint on aspects of our mind that are less conscious or less controlled and more spontaneous, that doesn't necessarily show up as much explicitly.
我们所知道的是，它们有两个共同的来源。一是它们在很大程度上反映了我们对整个社会等级制度的知识和内化，即在社会中谁拥有更多权力、谁拥有更高地位、谁受到更积极的看待。因此，这也是为什么它们有时会与我们的显性偏见产生分歧的部分原因，对吧？当我们看到所有这些刻板印象都在强化某些种族等级制度时，比如白人是强大、高地位和受崇拜的，而黑人是危险、犯罪和暴力的，这会在我们较少意识、较少控制、更自发的思维层面留下印记，而这并不一定在显性层面上表现得那么多。

23
00:19:25.000 --> 00:19:59.000
So that's one part of it. But we also know that personal experience and our personal affiliations matter a lot. So the largest systematic difference in Pacific racial bias we see between groups is that between white folks and black folks. And a large part of why that is is because, you know, if you are a white person in America, right, you're more likely to have white family, white friends, white significant others, your own sense of yourself as a white individual. and all of those things feed into a sense of favoritism for one's own group and a motivation to like things that are affiliated with one's own group.
所以这是其中的一部分。但我们也知道，个人经历和个人归属感非常重要。我们在不同群体之间看到的内隐种族偏见的最大系统性差异，是白人群体与黑人群体之间的差异。造成这种情况的很大一部分原因是，如果你是美国的白人，对吧，你更有可能有白人家人、白人朋友、白人重要另一半，你作为一个白人个体的自我意识，以及所有这些都会助长对自身群体的好感，以及喜欢与自身群体相关的事务的动机。

24
00:19:59.000 --> 00:21:02.000
You see that black folks also have, you know, a set of experiences that encourage favoritism for one's own group as well. And so when you put those two things together, that explains a lot of the variation in implicit racial biases across societies. So are we living in a time now where what had been implicit biases are coming to the fore and they're becoming explicit because there's so much dog whistling going on out there that it's giving people permission to act out what they would normally not? So I see, and this is me more speculating. Maybe it's not a fair question, but I ask it anyway. It's a great question. So I'm going to just put a little bit of grains of salt here because this is just my opinion on the matter. But I think that there have been a couple changes over the past four or five years that I think have really changed the nature of how racial bias is expressed in the United States.
你会发现，黑人群体也有一套鼓励对自己群体有好感的经历。因此，当你把这两件事放在一起时，就解释了社会间内隐种族偏见的很多变异。那么，我们现在是否生活在这样一个时代：过去曾是内隐偏见的现象正在浮出水面，并变得显性化，因为外面有太多的暗号（dog whistling），这给了人们许可去表现出他们平时不会表现出来的行为？所以，我看到——这更多是我在推测。也许这不是一个公平的问题，但我还是问了。这是一个很好的问题。因此，我将对此持保留态度，因为这只是我对此事的个人看法。但我认为，过去四五年里发生了一些变化，我认为这些变化确实改变了美国种族偏见表达的性质。

